" (https://www.ieee-whispers.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/11/whispers_2014_technical_program.pdf)\nciteturn163view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 94; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn160view2\",\"lineno\":3275}); Total lines: 3881\nL3227@P76: evaluation measures are applied to validate the ability of the suggested method for noise level reduction and for image quality en\u0002hancement. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed denoising algorithm achieves better performance when applied\nL3228@P76: on the suggested wavelet transformation compared to other examined transformation techniques.\nL3229@P76: Session fri-p-a : Noise reduction\nL3230@P76: Back to the main menu\nL3231@P76: Whispers Conference 2014, Lausanne, Switzerland\nL3233@P77: Session fri-p-a : Noise reduction\nL3234@P77: HyperSpectral Image Noise Reduction and its Effect on Spectral Unmixing\nL3235@P77: Azam Karami, iMinds- Visionlab, University of Antwerp, Belgium\nL3236@P77: Department of Electrical Engineering, Shahid Bahonar University of Kerman, Kerman, Iran\nL3237@P77: Rob Heylen and Paul Scheunders, iMinds- Visionlab, University of Antwerp, Belgium\nL3238@P77: Abstract : In hyperspectral images (HSI), many of the spectral bands have low noise levels (LN bands) while some have\nL3239@P77: high noise levels (junk bands). If a noise reduction algorithm is globally applied to the whole dataset, it usually affects the LN\nL3240@P77: bands adversely. Therefore, we consider different criteria for denoising LN and junk bands. First, we discriminate between LN and\nL3241@P77: junk bands using the spectral correlation between adjacent bands. After that, the mirror- extended curvelet transform is applied\nL3242@P77: to all spectral bands. Next, each LN band is denoised using a soft thresholding technique, while a local noise reduction method\nL3243@P77: is used for the junk bands, where the curvelet coefficients of adjacent LN bands are used to recover the junk bands. This targeted\nL3244@P77: approach is prone to reduce spectral distortions during denoising compared to global denoising methods. This is shown in simu\u0002lations where the proposed method is compared to a wavelet and a 3-dimensional Wiener filtering denoising algorithm. The\nL3245@P77: proposed method outperforms the global methods in terms of PSNR. To assess the effect of spectral distortion, spectral unmixing\nL3246@P77: is applied to the denoised results and reconstruction errors are compared, again showing the benefit of the proposed approach.\nL3247@P77: ESTIMATION OF CORRELATED NOISE IN HYPERSPECTRAL IMAGES\nL3248@P77: Asad Mahmood, Amandine Robin and Michael Sears,\nL3249@P77: School of Computer Science, University of the Witwatersrand, Johannesburg, South Africa\nL3250@P77: Abstract : Many hyperspectral image processing algorithms (e.g detection, classification, endmember extraction etc.) are\nL3251@P77: generally designed with the assumption of no spectral or spatial correlation in noise. However, studies have shown the presence\nL3252@P77: of non-negligible correlation between the noise samples in different spectral bands, especially between noise of adjacent bands.\nL3253@P77: It was also shown recently that many well- known endmember extraction algorithms e.g. give poor estimates for the number of\nL3254@P77: endmembers in the presence of correlated noise. This asks for a precise estimation of noise for cases where noise is spectrally\nL3255@P77: correlated. We show in this paper that the commonly employed hyperspectral noise estimation algorithm based on regression\nL3256@P77: residuals is very affected by spectrally correlated noise and we suggest a modified approach that proves to be very robust to noise\nL3257@P77: correlation. Simulation results show that the estimation error is reduced at times by a factor of 5 when there is high spectral cor\u0002relation in the noise.\nL3258@P77: APPLICATION OF NON-LINEAR PRINCIPAL COMPONENT ANALYSIS TO HYPERSPECTRAL DATA FOR NOISE\nL3259@P77: FILTERING USING ARTIFICIAL NEURAL NETWORKS\nL3260@P77: A. Piscini, Istituto Nazionale di Geofisica e Vulcanologia, Roma, Italy\nL3261@P77: G. Licciardi, GIPSA-lab, Grenoble-INP, Saint Martin d’He`res, France\nL3262@P77: Abstract : This work describes results of noise filtering techniques based on a Non-Linear Principal Component Analysis\nL3263@P77: (NLPCA), applied to Hyperspectral CASI remote sensed data. NLPCA was carried out using Artificial Neural Networks (ANN)\nL3264@P77: which are capable of modeling data non-linearity. NLPCA operated feature extraction on which original data were reconstructed,\nL3265@P77: filtering the noise which affects first VNIR bands. Such a technique cleans VNIR bands, but does not affect the signal of other\nL3266@P77: bands (SWIR). In order to appreciate improvements in classification due to noise filtering a supervised classification algorithm,\nL3267@P77: based on Support Vector Machines (SVM), was applied to both original, noise filtered data, and NLPCA components (NLPCs).\nL3268@P77: Results show that classification obtained using NLPCA-derived data is more accurate. Finally, SVM classification was also applied\nL3269@P77: to fused images adding LiDAR Digital Surface Model (DSM) to the NLPCs and the original image in order to evaluate further\nL3270@P77: improvements introduced by the dimensionality reduction offered by the NLPCA. Results show that the proposed method is able\nL3271@P77: to improve the classification both by reducing dimensionality and suppressing the noise.\nL3272@P77: Back to the main menu\nL3273@P77: Whispers Conference 2014, Lausanne, Switzerland\nL3274@P77-78: 78Opening of the conference\nL3275@P78: Session fri-p-b : Classification\nL3276@P78: XX. Session fri-p-b : Classification\nL3277@P78: COLLABORATIVE REPRESENTATION BASED K-NEAREST NEIGHBOR CLASSIFIER FOR HYPERSPECTRAL IMAGERY\nL3278@P78: Wei Li, College of Information Science & Technology, Beijing University of Chemical Technology\nL3279@P78: Qian Du, Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Mississippi State University\nL3280@P78: Fan Zhang and Wei Hu, College of Information Science & Technology, Beijing University of Chemical Technology\nL3281@P78: Abstract : We propose a novel collaborative representation based k- nearest neighbors algorithm for hyperspectral image\nL3282@P78: classification. The proposed method is based on a collaborative representation computed by an l\nL3283@P78: 2\nL3284@P78: -norm minimization with a Tik\u0002honov regularization matrix. More specific, the testing sample is represented as a linear combination of all the training samples,\nL3285@P78: and the weights for representation are estimated by an l\nL3286@P78: 2\nL3287@P78: -norm minimization derived closed-form solution. The label of the testing\nL3288@P78: sample is determined by the majority vote of those with k largest representation weights. The experimental results show that the\nL3289@P78: proposed algorithm achieves better performance than several previous algorithms, such as the original k-nearest neighbor classi\u0002fier and local mean based nearest neighbor classifier.\nL3290@P78: AN IMPROVED MARKER SELECTION METHOD FOR HYPERSPECTRAL IMAGE SEGMENTATION AND CLASSIFICATION\nL3291@P78: Davood Akbari, Dept. of Surveying and Geomatics Eng., College of Engineering, University of Tehran, Tehran, Iran\nL3292@P78: Saeid Homayouni, Department of Geography, University of Ottawa, Ottawa, Canada\nL3293@P78: Abdolreza Safari, Dept. of Surveying and Geomatics Eng., College of Engineering, University of Tehran, Tehran, Iran\nL3294@P78: Safa Khazai, Dept. of Civil Eng., College of Engineering, Imam Hussein Comprehensive University, Tehran, Iran\nL3295@P78: Hossein Torabzadeh, Remote Sensing Laboratories, University of Zurich, Zurich, Switzerland\nL3296@P78: Abstract : Recently, a new approach for spectral-spatial classification of hyperspectral images has been proposed by Tara\u0002balka et al. This approach is based on the Minimum Spanning Forest (MSF) grown from automatically selected markers by using\nL3297@P78: the Support Vector Machines (SVM) classification. This paper aims at improving this approach by means of a new method for\nL3298@P78: the selection of markers. This method is a combination of SVM and multi-layer perceptron (MLP) neural network classifiers. In\nL3299@P78: the proposed method, the most reliable pixels, i.e. markers, are extracted from the classification maps and used to build the MSF.\nL3300@P78: Three scenarios are evaluated for the first stage of marker selection: SVM, MPL and combination of SVM and MPL. Experimental\nL3301@P78: results on two benchmark hyperspectral datasets demonstrate that the proposed method significantly improves the classification\nL3302@P78: accuracies compared to the approach based on the SVM classification.\nL3303@P78: A NOVEL SEMISUPERVISED TRANSDUCTIVE SVM WITH SPATIAL SIMILARITY FOR CLASSIFICATION OF HY\u0002PERSPECTRAL DATA\nL3304@P78: Lian-Zhi Huo, Institute of Remote Sensing and Digital Earth Chinese Academy of Sciences Beijing, 100101, P. R. China\nL3305@P78: Li-Jun Zhao, Institute of Remote Sensing and Digital Earth Chinese Academy of Sciences Beijing, 100101, P. R. China\nL3306@P78: University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, 100049, P. R. China\nL3307@P78: Ping Tang, Institute of Remote Sensing and Digital Earth Chinese Academy of Sciences Beijing, 100101, P. R. China\nL3308@P78: Abstract : In this paper, a novel semisupervised algorithm is proposed with spatial similarity for classification of hyper\u0002spectral data based on the transductive support vector machines algorithm. The proposed method exploits the characteristic of\nL3309@P78: the hyperspectral data, in which spatially nearby points are likely to belong to the same class. To utilize this assumption, a novel\nL3310@P78: transductive support vector machines algorithm is proposed. Experimental tests are performed on two hyperspectral data sets.\nL3311@P78: The results show that the proposed algorithm obtains\nL3312@P78: Back to the main menu\nL3313@P78: Whispers Conference 2014, Lausanne, Switzerland\nL3314@P78-79: 79Opening of the conference\nL3315@P79: Session fri-p-b : Classification\nL3316@P79: Support Tensor Machine with Local Pixel Neighborhood for Hyperspectral Image Classification\nL3317@P79: Xian Guo, Xin Huang and Liangpei Zhang,\nL3318@P79: The State Key Laboratory of Information Engineering in Surveying, Mapping, and Remote Sensing, Wuhan University, P.R. China\nL3319@P79: Lefei Zhang, School of Computer, Wuhan University, P.R. China\nL3320@P79: Abstract : A multiclass support tensor machine (STM) for the classification of remotely-sensed imagery is investigated in\nL3321@P79: this study aiming at simultaneously exploiting the spectral and spatial information for accurate image interpretation. Spatial rela\u0002tionship of neighboring pixels has been taken into consideration by a local pixel neighborhood (LPN), which processes the local\nL3322@P79: imagery patch as a cube, and is capable of separating land classes in both spectral and spatial domains. To deal with the tensor data\nL3323@P79: and keep the data structure in high-order feature space, support vector machine has been extended to support tensor machine\nL3324@P79: by the multilinear algebra. Experiments conducted on AVIRIS hyperspectral image revealed that the STM achieved much better\nL3325@P79: results than the standard SVM classifier.\nL3326@P79: CONTRIBUTION OF BAND SELECTION AND FUSION FOR HYPERSPECTRAL CLASSIFICATION\nL3327@P79: Nesrine Chehata, IRD/UMR LISAH El Menzah 4, Tunis, Tunisia\nL3328@P79: EA 4592 G&E, ENSEGID-IPB, University of Bordeaux, 1, allée F. Daguin, 33 607 Pessac Cedex, France\nL3329@P79: Arnaud Le Bris, Université Paris-Est, IGN/SR, MATIS, 73 avenue de Paris, 94160 Saint Mandé, France\nL3330@P79: Safa Najjar, ENSI Ecole Nationale des Sciences Informatiques, La mannouba, Tunisie\nL3331@P79: Abstract : For some specific land cover classification problems, it may be interesting to design superspectral camera sys\u0002tems with reduced numbers of bands (~20) and optimized band widths. This paper assesses the contribution of band selection and\nL3332@P79: band fusion processes separately and jointly for dimensionality reduction. The proposed approach is fully automatic and based on\nL3333@P79: a wrapper feature selection using Random forest classifier and a similarity-based fusion process. While combining both processes,\nL3334@P79: selection before fusion gave the best results, reducing by almost 91% the number of bands while keeping satisfying accuracies.\nL3335@P79: Results are presented on Indian Pines, Salinas and Pavia Centre hyperspectral datasets.\nL3336@P79: EDGE CONSTRAINED MRF METHOD FOR CLASSIFICATION OF HYPERSPECTRAL IMAGERY\nL3337@P79: Li Ni, Bing Zhang, Qian Shen, Lianru Gao, Xu Sun and Shanshan Li,\nL3338@P79: Key Laboratory of Digital Earth Science, Institute of Remote Sensing and Digital Earth, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, China\nL3339@P79: Hua Wu, Institute of Geographic Sciences and Natural Resources Research, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, China\nL3340@P79: Abstract : This paper proposes an edge constrained Markov random field (MRF) method for accurate classification\nL3341@P79: of hyperspectral imagery. The characteristic of the proposed method is using an alterable spatial weighting coefficient, which is\nL3342@P79: acquired based on the edge information, instead of a traditional fixed coefficient. In this way, the spatial contributions in MRF\nL3343@P79: model are different for pixels inside objects or on the boundaries. Therefore, the “salt and pepper” inside object can be removed\nL3344@P79: and the “overcorrection” can be resolved. Experimental results of the synthetic hyperspectral data and the real hyperspectral\nL3345@P79: image demonstrate that the proposed method works better on both homogeneous regions and class boundaries with improved\nL3346@P79: classification accuracies.\nL3347@P79: CLASSIFICATION OF ENERGY TREE SPECIES USING SUPPORT VECTOR MACHINES\nL3348@P79: Peter Burai, Laszlo Beko, Csaba Lenart and Tamas Tomor,\nL3349@P79: Karoly Robert College, Research Institute of Remote Sensing and Rural Development, Hungary\nL3350@P79: Abstract : This paper investigates whether the combination of airborne hyperspectral imagery (Aisa EAGLE II) and\nL3351@P79: image classification methods (MLC,SVM) using feature extraction can discriminate among species and clones of energy trees.\nL3352@P79: The trees examined have similar morphological traits due to limitation of detection. The image classification was applied on a\nL3353@P79: spectrally selected and transformed (PCA, MNF) dataset. A binary tree SVM classifier was developed in accordance with the\nL3354@P79: principle of SVM, based on the Jeffries-Matusita (JM) separability measure of selected classes. The adaptive binary tree SVM on\nL3355@P79: MNF-transformed dataset provided more accurate results than applied MLC and multiclass SVM methods. The primary outcome\nL3356@P79: of this study was a comparison of support vector machines (SVM) classification methods to evaluate species or clones of energy\nL3357@P79: plants. In this paper, an adaptive binary tree SVM classifier (ABTSVM) is proposed to increase the accuracy of subspecies level.\nL3358@P79: Back to the main menu\nL3359@P79: Whispers Conference 2014, Lausanne, Switzerland\nL3360@P79-80: 80Opening of the conference\nL3361@P80: Session fri-p-b : Classification\nL3362@P80: A NOVEL SPECTRAL SPATIAL FILTERING APPROACH FOR HYPERSPECTRAL IMAGE CLASSIFICATION\nL3363@P80: Kun Shang, State Key Laboratory of Remote Sensing Sciences, Institute of Remote Sensing and Digital Earth,\nL3364@P80: Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, China\nL3365@P80: University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, China\nL3366@P80: Xia Zhang, State Key Laboratory of Remote Sensing Sciences, Institute of Remote Sensing and Digital Earth,\nL3367@P80: Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100101, China\n"
